# UI v2 "Refined Native" — design specification

Status: C27 deliverable, reviewed at the 9/10 bar (D058). This document and
its companions ([tokens](tokens.md), [state ownership](state-ownership.md),
[invariants](invariants.md), [screens](screens/), [provenance](provenance.md))
are what every P09 chunk is judged against. The existing
[DESIGN.md](../DESIGN.md) remains authoritative for the native authority
interface, transport, Academy presentation ownership and the Harness; this
document changes only how the desktop presents those.

## 1. Scope and deliverable

The reviewed prototype is extracted in three layers, in order:

1. **Spec** (C27, this folder): tokens, screen inventory, state ownership,
   invariants, interaction contracts, copy, provenance, acceptance criteria.
2. **TypeScript presentation** (C28–C29, then per-destination chunks): the new
   shell and screens in `apps/desktop`, built against wire fixtures and the
   mock runtime, behind a Rust-owned `ui_variant` flag.
3. **Rust contract** (within each destination chunk): the intents, scopes and
   refusals the screens need, implemented over existing desktop-core services
   and exported through ts-rs.

The first implementation slice is the shell plus the first-run wizard and
add-tenant sheet (C28–C29). Harness, Inbox/reads, reviews/Administration, API
Client, Test Cases and the Academy embed follow (C30–C34). The legacy shell,
its feature-view switch, the flag and the legacy native acceptance scripts are
removed in C34; the phase is not complete while any destination renders only
in the legacy shell.

## 2. Frozen reference

Canvas https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL, page "Refined Native
v2", version 134. The six R2 files are copied verbatim under
[prototype/](prototype/) with SHA-256 hashes in `MANIFEST.sha256`. They are
immutable inputs. Codex scored the prototype 8/10 on rounds 4–16; the five
open round-16 items are carried as known gaps (§10) and closed in
implementation, not by further prototype rounds.

## 3. Architecture fit

- **Authority** stays with desktop-core (D014). The UI dispatches intents and
  observes scopes through the existing two-command Tauri bridge. Every
  prototype state field is assigned in [state-ownership.md](state-ownership.md);
  no persisted or tenant-sensitive state is UI-owned (UI state is transient
  presentation state only), and no state goes to browser storage.
- **Contract**: new intents, scopes and refusals are Rust types with ts-rs
  derives, regenerated by `xtask bindings`. A hand-written parser is added to
  the contracts client only where runtime validation beyond the type is needed.
- **Shell flag**: `ShellSnapshot.ui_variant` (`legacy` | `v2`) and
  `ShellSnapshot.onboarding` (`needed` | `inProgress` | `done`) are Rust-owned,
  fixture-able and seedable by native acceptance. The React root switches on
  them.
- **Navigation inventory**: one Rust list (feature ids with availability,
  label, shortcut index and sidebar membership) feeds the native menu, the
  sidebar and the command palette. Feature ids gain `home` and `inbox`;
  `library` is shown as "Test Cases"; `gateway` and `redTeam` render inside
  Administration's accordion; `sessions`, `import` and `settings` stay
  reachable from the palette and Administration.
- **Shortcuts** ⌘1–⌘9 are defined by the native menu, which dispatches
  `navigate`; the sidebar displays the menu's truth.
- **Transient state**: a small zustand store per screen family (shell, wizard,
  inbox, admin, harness, workbench); React state only for form fields.
- **Tokens**: local `--v2-*` custom properties ([tokens.md](tokens.md));
  Academy variables untouched for the embedded learner view.
- **Copy**: one `copy.ts` per screen family exporting constants; tests assert
  exact strings. The voice is the wizard's: this app is built entirely on
  Palo Alto Networks' public APIs and exists to show application builders what
  those interfaces make possible; it explains the API call as you go.
- **Show the calls**: a Rust-owned preference reveals, for each action, the
  request and response as the diagnostics module already redacts them
  (credential ids in place of secrets, a note that the core attaches the real
  header). Default on until onboarding completes.

## 4. Layout rhythm

Shared by every destination, the wizard and the add-tenant sheet:

- 32px top and 44px side page margins; 26px/700 titles; 18–20px card
  padding; 10–12px gaps; 32px controls; 14px body, 12px secondary (11px only
  pills, eyebrows and mono badges); 860px reading cap; 244px rail (first-run
  wizard rail 320px).
- Minimum window 1180×720. Below 1280 wide the rail collapses to icons with
  tooltips; nothing scrolls horizontally.
- Tool workspaces (Harness, API Client) have a title row like every other
  destination; no destination is exempt from the rhythm.

## 5. Destinations

| Sidebar | Feature id | Shortcut | Contract |
|---|---|---|---|
| Home | `home` (new) | ⌘1 | [screens/shell.md](screens/shell.md) |
| Inbox | `inbox` (new) | ⌘2 | [screens/inbox-reads.md](screens/inbox-reads.md) |
| Harness | `harness` | ⌘3 | [screens/harness.md](screens/harness.md) |
| Academy | `academy` | ⌘4 | [screens/academy.md](screens/academy.md) |
| Test Cases | `library` | ⌘5 | [screens/api-client-test-cases.md](screens/api-client-test-cases.md) |
| API Client | `apiClient` | ⌘6 | same |
| Administration | `administration` (+ `gateway`, `redTeam` inside) | ⌘7 | [screens/reviews-administration.md](screens/reviews-administration.md) |
| Tenant switcher | — | ⌘T | [screens/shell.md](screens/shell.md) |
| Command palette | — | ⌘K | same |
| First-run wizard, add-tenant sheet | — | — | [screens/wizard.md](screens/wizard.md) |

Red Team is read-only everywhere. Gamification appears only inside the Academy
embed. Product availability is governed by §6.

## 6. Products, admitted services and the unsupported boundary

The prototype shows six products. Only a product with an admitted service in
desktop-core is verified, inventoried or changed; the rest render as
unavailable. Nothing in P09 adds a product API.

| Product | Admitted reads | Admitted changes | P09 treatment |
|---|---|---|---|
| AI Runtime Security | `ManagementResource` (profiles, topics, applications, API keys, deployment profiles, DLP catalog/profiles/patterns, filtering profiles, dictionaries, sessions); scan (`scan.rs`) | `ChangeKind` Profiles, Topics, Applications, ApiKeys, DataPatterns, DataProfiles, FilteringProfiles, Dictionaries with `ChangeAction` Create/Version/Rotate/Delete/… (`changes.rs`) | Full: Administration tabs, reviews, Playground, Inbox items |
| AI Gateway | `GatewayRead` (inventory, detail, integration models, prerequisites, observe, MCP servers) | Gateway `ChangeKind`s (workspaces, providers, routes, guardrails, service/user keys, integrations) | Full, inside Administration; Harness lifecycle 2 reads `McpServers` |
| AI Red Teaming | `red_team.rs` reads (scan inventory, statistics, static reports; C13) | none (execution stays a disabled preview) | Read-only card and tabs; Test Cases "Inspect" only |
| Model/skill observability (AI Supply Chain in the prototype) | C13 model/skill reads | none | Read-only; shown under its C13 name, never as a scanning product |
| Agentic Identity Security | none | none | **Not available in this release**: card and accordion entry with the dependency gate ("needs an admitted AIS service; see D058"); not selectable in the wizard; never verified; no inventory, no policy review |
| Agentic Endpoint Security | none | none | Same as AIS; the prototype's AES policy review, discovered-AI table and warning demo are fixtures only and are not implemented |

The per-tab and per-action mapping, including the restricted Runtime and
Gateway change matrices (for example no application Create, no API-key
Update, no data-profile Delete) and every removed or unavailable prototype
tab, is enumerated in
[screens/administration-inventory.md](screens/administration-inventory.md).

Rules: an unavailable product or tab never produces a verification result, an
Inbox item, an inventory row or a review. The wizard's verify step lists it
as "Not available" (not pass, warning or fail). Test V2-T19 asserts that the
admin inventory for an unavailable product has no tabs and no actions, and
V2-R10 asserts that no intent exists that would mutate it; V2-T20 and V2-R11
cover unavailable tabs and denied actions within admitted products.

## 7. Error presentation

- **Refusal** (core refused an intent): warn toast with the refusal reason and
  the tenant named; never a modal. Guard-type refusals (tenant switch with a
  pending decision) render their dialog from the refusal payload.
- **Delivery unknown**: inline "not confirmed" state on the control that
  dispatched, with Retry; the control is not reset.
- **Contract violation**: generic warn toast; detail to diagnostics.

## 8. Accessibility additions

The native acceptance checklist gains four items that every P09 chunk's
acceptance script asserts: accent-colour focus ring on every focusable
control (inset variant where declared); `role="status"` live regions for read
status, API send completion and review apply; one topmost modal with
`aria-hidden` siblings and focus return; toasts persist under Reduce Motion.
Existing requirements (keyboard-only, VoiceOver attributes, 150%/200% zoom,
contrast AA) continue to apply to every changed screen.

## 9. Budgets

Existing `budgets.json` entries carry unchanged. New: a tenant switch renders
the new tenant's Home from cached scopes within 100 ms, with read status
showing pending until the read lands (measured by the C30 workload).

## 10. Known gaps carried from the prototype

Codex round 16 (score 8) left five items, closed by the chunk named:

1. Secret disclosure survives a tenant switch with the key review open → I6, C31.
2. Applied injection-override examples show "Not demonstrated" → topic
   verdicts keyed separately from detectors, profile-aware, C31.
3. Administration creation labels from a per-product single value → per-tab
   `AdminInventory` capability and label, C31.
4. Home snooze tooltip hard-codes "Snooze 24 hours" → shared item
   `snoozeLabel`, I11, C30.
5. API Client Send announces nothing → `role="status"` completion region, C33.

## 11. Verification

- Behaviour: Vitest + Testing Library component tests, one named fixture per
  prototype wrapper board; the board → initial props → test id table is in
  [screens/boards.md](screens/boards.md), so reviewed-state coverage can be
  audited without reconstructing canvas wrappers.
- Contract: Rust integration tests per invariant (`V2-Rxx`).
- Native: the existing Accessibility-API scripts, extended per chunk, plus a
  screenshot evidence set per chunk at 100% and 200% zoom in both themes filed
  under `evidence/Cxx/screens/`.
- Live: only C29 (wizard verify against Vulture, reads only) and C31 (review
  apply against Redtail under the `airs-v2-c25-` prefix) run live, inside the
  scope the owner designated on 2026-10-25. Every other chunk runs on fixtures
  and the mock runtime.
- Review: C27 at 9/10 in every dimension, enforced by the controller (a
  `docs_only` chunk refuses owner acceptance and needs no native
  preparation); C28–C34 may complete at 8 under D034 and are raised to 9
  before the P09 phase review.
- Legacy native scripts removed in C34 are first mapped, assertion by
  assertion, to replacement native-check ids (C34 milestone 2), so the
  cumulative P09 gate keeps exercising every earlier authority and
  accessibility requirement.
# Normative invariants

These twelve rules are the behaviours Codex forced into the prototype over
sixteen review rounds. They are normative: an implementation chunk that
violates one fails its behaviour or authority dimension regardless of how the
screen looks. Code shape is free; the rule and its test are not.

Each rule names its acceptance test id. `V2-Txx` tests are Vitest component
tests in the desktop app (fixture-driven); `V2-Rxx` are Rust integration tests
in desktop-core; `V2-Nxx` are native acceptance checks added to
`native-checks.json` by the chunk that implements the rule.

| Id | Rule | Tests | Chunk |
|---|---|---|---|
| I1 | There is exactly one tenant-switch path. The core refuses a switch while a Harness decision is pending; the UI renders the archive-or-discard guard from the refusal and re-dispatches with the chosen resolution. | V2-R01 refusal on pending decision; V2-T01 guard rendered from refusal payload; V2-N01 switch via switcher, palette and menu all pass through the guard | C30 |
| I2 | Every observed scope is per tenant. Switching tenants never shows another tenant's Inbox items, reads, reviews, conversations, drafts or rotation state. | V2-R02 scope isolation across two tenants; V2-T02 switcher round-trip leaves per-tenant transient state intact | C30 |
| I3 | A review is bound to the tenant and resource it was opened for and applies only when the typed name matches, validated in the core. A tenant change invalidates the review. | V2-R03 confirmReview refuses on mismatch and on changed context; V2-T03 Apply disabled until names match | C31 |
| I4 | Reads are finite. The UI distinguishes "first read pending" from "no activity", reports per-product partial coverage, and the completion toast is warn-kind whenever any source is partial. | V2-R04 ReadStatus transitions; V2-T04 toast kind and text per coverage; V2-N02 pending state visible on first run | C30 |
| I5 | API responses are immutable snapshots. A later configuration change never alters an earlier response in history; a newly sent request reflects the change. | V2-R05 history entries hash-stable; V2-T05 topic applied → old response unchanged, new response includes it | C33 |
| I6 | An issued secret is disclosed only through the host (`RevealSecret` sheet, host clipboard), is held per result for the active context and never persisted; any review exit, including a tenant switch or context retirement, drops it (`ForgetSecret`) and resets the reveal state. A dropped secret is not recoverable; the UI offers to issue another key. | V2-R06 held key dropped on review exit, switch and retirement; Reveal after drop refuses; V2-T06 resumed rotation shows no secret controls | C31 |
| I7 | Key rotation is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback. Until the old key is removed, the reminder is a distinct Inbox item with its own identity and stated return time and the key table shows both keys. Two timestamps are stored once and labelled distinctly: deployment acknowledged (local statement) and old key removed (remote readback). A failed or unknown issuance never reissues automatically; a rejected or unknown Delete keeps the reminder and leaves the completion timestamp unset; unknown outcomes are shown as unconfirmed with reconciliation, never as complete. | V2-R07 state machine incl. failed/unknown issuance and rejected/unknown delete; V2-T07 Inbox item lifecycle; V2-T08 timestamps labelled and stable after refresh | C31 |
| I8 | One topmost modal. Siblings of the topmost dialog are `aria-hidden`; Escape closes only the topmost; focus returns to the opener. | V2-T09 stacking; V2-N03 keyboard-only dialog traversal | C28 |
| I9 | A verdict or severity is colour plus icon plus word, never colour alone. | V2-T10 every verdict renderer emits all three; V2-N04 accessibility tree carries the word | C28 |
| I10 | Toasts have a kind (ok, info, warn), expire on a timer, and are presentation only: everything a toast says is derivable from an observed scope. With Reduce Motion on, toasts persist until dismissed. | V2-T11 kinds and expiry; V2-N05 Reduce Motion persistence | C28 |
| I11 | Inbox acknowledge and snooze are per item, core-owned, with the return time stated wherever the action is offered (Home, Inbox row, Inbox detail) and identical across them. | V2-R08 snooze return computed in core; V2-T12 Home and Inbox labels equal | C30 |
| I12 | A fresh tenant never shows seeded or sample data. Its first Harness send creates a user conversation; its Inbox is empty until the first read lands. | V2-R09 fresh tenant scopes empty; V2-T13 first send creates conversation | C30, C32 |

Rules I1, I3, I6 and I7 are authority rules under D014: the core owns the
decision, the UI renders it. No invariant may be satisfied by invented data: a
product, tab or operation without an admitted service renders as unavailable
(DESIGN-V2 §6), and a test that passes only on fixtures for such a capability
is a specification defect.
# State ownership

Every state key the frozen prototype actually holds is listed here, by file
and by the name the prototype uses, with its owner, where it lives, its
lifetime, and whether the implementation keeps, renames, derives or drops it.

- **Core** — Rust-owned in desktop-core, exposed through `observe`
  (persisted in the store or session-held), never in browser storage.
- **UI** — transient presentation state in a per-screen-family zustand store
  (shell, wizard, inbox, admin, harness, workbench) or React form state.
  Lost on reload by design.
- **Host** — never leaves the native host (secret sheet, clipboard).
- **Dropped** — not implemented; the reason is in [provenance.md](provenance.md).

The inventory was produced from `grep -o` over `s.*`, `ts.*` and `f.*` in
`prototype/R2App.dc.html` and `prototype/R2Onboard.dc.html` (version 134)
and reconciled by hand; a key missing here is a specification defect.

## R2Onboard — wizard and add-tenant sheet (`s.*`, `s.f.*`)

| Prototype key | Meaning | Owner | Where / intent | Lifetime | Disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `s.idx`, `s.path`, `s.vi` | current step, step path, visited steps | UI | wizard store | until the wizard closes | kept; `onboarding=inProgress` is set in core when the welcome step is left |
| `s.f.name` | tenant display name | UI → Core | `completeOnboarding`/`addTenant` | written at completion | kept |
| `s.env`, `s.region` | environment, region selects | UI → Core | same | same | kept |
| `s.f.tsg` | TSG id (10 digits) | UI → Core | same | same | kept |
| `s.f.clientId`, `s.f.secret` | management OAuth client id and secret (required in the prototype) | id: UI → Core; secret: **Host** | id in `addTenant`; secret entered only in the host secret sheet, vault record id returned | secret never in the web layer | **changed** (deviation P17): management credentials become an optional second section under D013; the id is kept, the secret field is replaced by "Add management client…" |
| `s.scanOn`, `s.f.scanKey` | scan key toggle and value | toggle: UI; key: **Host** | "Add scan key…" opens the host secret sheet; required for first run (D013) | never in the web layer | **changed** (P1, P17): toggle removed on first run (the scan key is required); kept in add mode |
| `s.gwOn`, `s.f.ws`, `s.f.endpoint`, `s.f.gwKey` | Gateway section toggle, workspace name, endpoint URL, workspace key | toggle/ws/endpoint: UI → Core; key: **Host** | `addTenant.gateway{ws, endpoint}`; key via secret sheet | — | kept; key path changed (P1) |
| `s.tried`, `s.vsig`, `s.vsStale` | validation attempted, validation signature, stale flag | UI (tried) / Core (signature, stale) | wizard component; `setup.verify.stale`, `setup.completable` | per step / per draft | kept; the signature and the completion gate moved to the core so the presentation cannot complete over stale results |
| `s.vs` | verify results per product | Core | `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify` (streamed `VerifyStep`s from `verifyTenantSetup`, with `done`/`failed`/`stale`) | until the draft completes or is discarded | kept; computed in core, including staleness against a signature of the draft's inputs |
| `s.aesFixed`, `s.failCleared` | AES warning demo acknowledged; failed row cleared | UI | wizard store | per run | **changed**: AES is not an admitted product (P18); the warning demo becomes a generic "warning row" fixture on an admitted product |
| `s.prods` | selected products | UI → Core | `completeOnboarding.products` | written at completion | kept; unavailable products are not selectable (P18) |
| `s.packs`, `s.casesState` | starter packs selected, pack state | UI → Core | `completeTenantSetup.products` → the bundled catalog's cases as library entries named by their starter pack, Not run (P55) | written at completion | kept |
| `s.acad`, `s.af` (`f.email`, `f.dname`), `s.streaks`, `s.remind` | local Academy account form, streaks, reminders | **Dropped** / Core (Academy) | Academy identity is hosted (D004–D006, C04); the step signs in or skips | — | **changed** (P19): no local account, streaks or reminders in the wizard |
| `s.help` | help popover open | UI | wizard store | transient | kept |
| `s.opened` | "Open Prisma AIRS" progress | UI | mirrors the `onboarding` transition | transient | kept |
| `s.theme` | theme prop | Core | `appearance` | — | kept (observed) |

## R2App — shell (`s.*`)

| Prototype key | Meaning | Owner | Where | Lifetime | Disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `s.tenant`, `s.byTenant` | active tenant; per-tenant state bag | Core | `ShellSnapshot.active`, per-tenant scopes below | persisted | kept; the bag is replaced by scopes |
| `s.screen` | destination | Core | `navigate` intent, `ShellSnapshot.features` | session | kept; feature ids gain `home`, `inbox` |
| `s.theme` | theme | Core | `appearance` | persisted | kept |
| `s.wizard`, `s.added` | first-run wizard open; add-tenant sheet result | Core / UI | `ShellSnapshot.onboarding`; sheet result is a toast | — | kept |
| `s.overlayDone` | prototype overlay bookkeeping | **Dropped** | canvas-only | — | dropped |
| `s.palette`, `s.pq` | palette open, query | UI | shell store | transient | kept |
| `s.tenantOpen`, `s.guard` | switcher open; switch guard | UI | shell store; guard content from the `SwitchBlocked` refusal (`to`, `pending`, `completion`), rendered by the shell for a switch and by the wizard for a setup completion | transient | kept (I1); completion is the same guarded path (P36) |
| `s.toast`, `s.toastKind`, `s.undo` | toast text, kind, undo target | UI | shell store, timer | ≤ 6 s | kept (I10) |
| `s.detail`, `s.sheet` | detail sheet; confirm sheet | UI | shell store modal stack | transient | kept (I8) |
| `s.explain` | explain-mode toggle | UI | shell store | transient | kept |
| `s.sel`, `s.itab` | Inbox selection, tab | UI | shell store, keyed by tenant id | transient; a switch round trip keeps it (I2) | kept |
| `s.product`, `s.exp`, `s.ptab`, `s.allOpen`, `s.adminNav`, `s.adminQ`, `s.tree` | Administration product, expanded set, tab per product, expand-all, nav mode, search, tree | UI | admin store | transient | kept |
| `s.review`, `s.reviewKind`, `s.reviewFrom`, `s.reviewTenant`, `s.reviewApplied` | review open, kind, origin, bound tenant, applied | Core | `Review` scope (tenant-bound, invalidated on context change) | session | kept; the UI holds only "open" |
| `s.typed` | typed confirmation | UI | admin store | until review exit | kept (I3) |
| `s.secretVisible` | reveal toggle | **Host** | `RevealSecret` sheet; UI holds nothing | 60 s sheet | **changed** (P2): no web reveal state |
| `s.credOpen`, `s.credVal` | credential dialog open, value | Host | credential entry is the host secret sheet | — | **changed** (P1): no web credential value |
| `s.packsOpen`, `s.packPick` | starter-pack dialog, picks | UI → Core | library intent | transient | kept |
| `s.ncOpen`, `s.ncName`, `s.ncPid`, `s.ncInput`, `s.ncDir` | new-case dialog fields | UI → Core | `createCase` (existing library) | transient | kept |
| `s.casePage`, `s.caseQ`, `s.cfil` | Test Cases paging, search, filter | UI | Test Cases screen state (keyed by context) | transient | kept |
| `s.req`, `s.reqQ`, `s.fmt`, `s.rtab`, `s.rsub` | API Client request, search, body format, response tab, sub-tab | UI | workbench store | transient | kept |
| `s.quiz` | Academy quiz state | **Dropped** | hosted Academy owns it (P19) | — | dropped |
| `s.convoHint` | ended-conversation hint | UI | harness store; cleared by any navigation; its opening question is the core's `opening` (the conversation's, or the opened saved record's), never the title (P51) | transient | kept |
| (none; DESIGN-V2 §3) | "Show the calls" preference and panel | Core | `ShellSnapshot.showCalls` (the operator's choice once made, else on until onboarding completes) and `ShellSnapshot.calls` (the active tenant's calls this app run: request line, credential kind, header names, status, duration; never a value, query or body) (P54) | preference persisted; calls in memory for the app run | added |
| `ts.activeCase` hand-off | the case opened in the Playground | UI → Core | shell store `playground` (tenant, case, fields) → Runtime Playground prefill; the scan names the case (`scan.case`) and the core records its result (P55, P56) | until the next hand-off | kept |

## R2App — per-tenant (`ts.*`)

| Prototype key | Meaning | Owner | Where | Lifetime | Disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `ts.reading`, `ts.reads`, `ts.firstRead`, `ts.lastRead`, `ts.warnCleared`, `ts.capsScan` | read in progress, count, first read done, last-read time, warning cleared, scan capability | Core | `ReadStatus` scope, from the tenant's read record (one per opened tenant, kept across a switch round trip, P37) | session, per tenant | kept (I4); `reads` count is not shown and never feeds a timestamp |
| `ts.ack`, `ts.snoozed` | acknowledgments, snoozes with return | Core | `InboxEntry.hold` (`acknowledge`, or `snooze` with `returnsAt` computed in the core); intents `acknowledgeItem`/`snoozeItem`/`unsnoozeItem`; the last snooze's `SnoozeReceipt` on the Inbox scope is what the toast announces, once (P38) | session, per tenant (as v1's holds) | kept (I11); not persisted across a relaunch, as the retained holds were not |
| `ts.prompt`, `ts.dir`, `ts.profile`, `ts.model`, `ts.app`, `ts.kind`, `ts.scanned`, `ts.scanDir`, `ts.scanProfile`, `ts.results`, `ts.activeCase`, `ts.activeInput`, `ts.activeDir` | Playground prompt, direction, profile, model, app, classified kind, scanned flag, scan selectors, per-case results, active case | prompt/dir/profile/model/app: UI; scan result + per-case results: Core | workbench store; `ScanRecordView` (existing) and library case results | prompt transient; results persisted per tenant | kept; verdict computed by the existing scan path, never by a UI classifier (the prototype's `classify` is fixture-only) |
| `ts.extraCases`, `ts.packs` | user-created cases, packs | Core | library | persisted | kept |
| `ts.hist` | API responses | Core | workbench history | persisted | kept (I5) |
| `ts.topicRev`, `ts.topicDesc`, `ts.injApplied`, `ts.aesApplied`, `ts.aesRev` | applied review results | Core | read back from the product scopes after apply | — | kept for Runtime topics; `aes*` **dropped** (P18) |
| `ts.keyRotated`, `ts.keySecretClosed`, `ts.keyDeployed`, `ts.keyDoneAt` | rotation stage | Core | `Tenant.rotations` → `ShellSnapshot.rotations: RotationView{stage, oldKey, newKey, issuedBy, deploymentAcknowledgedAt, oldKeyRemovedAt, removalRejected}`; stage derived (issuanceUnconfirmed, issued, resumed, acknowledged, removalUnconfirmed, complete); intent `acknowledgeDeployment` | per tenant, persisted (timestamps stored once, and the reviewed key name that alone confirms an unconfirmed issuance); the held secret only on its change result, for its context | **changed** (P20, P41): supported Version → Delete sequence; see screens/reviews-administration.md |
| `ts.follows` | follow-up status per conversation | Core | sessions | persisted | kept |
| `ts.convo`, `ts.userConvos`, `ts.msgsBy`, `ts.decs`, `ts.ended`, `ts.archived` | current conversation, user conversations, messages, decisions, ended flags, archives | Core | existing harness sessions and decisions | persisted, protected | kept |
| `ts.draft` | composer draft | UI | shell store per tenant and workspace ID (`harnessDrafts`), with kept messages (`harnessUnsent`: not sent, or not confirmed until their own conversation reports the core took their send ID, `ConversationView.submissions`) | transient | kept |

## Credential entry paths (native)

| Credential | Entry | Storage | Readable by the web layer |
|---|---|---|---|
| Scan API key | webview password field read through a ref, cleared at once, sent once as `EnteredSecret` inside `configureScanCredential` (P25) | vault (`cr-` record), Keychain v2 namespace | state and mode only (`ScanView`), never the value |
| Management client secret | same path, `configureManagementCredential` (optional section) | vault | client id and state only |
| Gateway workspace key | same path, `configureInferenceCredential` | vault | state only |
| Issued API key (rotation) | `RevealSecret` host sheet / host clipboard | held per result for the active context, never persisted; retired by the review's exit (`DiscardChange` on the applied review, also before the change settles), by acknowledging the deployment, or by a context change | never |
cat: docs/architecture/ui-v2/boards.md: No such file or directory
# Provenance and intentional deviations

Every fixture file and screen contract cites the frozen prototype file and the
prototype state names it derives from. This file lists where the
implementation deliberately differs from the prototype and why. Anything not
listed here is expected to match the prototype's behaviour and spacing.

Frozen reference: canvas https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL,
page "Refined Native v2", version 134 (version id 1793059808-4929, published
2026-10-26). Files and hashes: [prototype/MANIFEST.sha256](prototype/MANIFEST.sha256).

| # | Prototype | Implementation | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| P1 | Wizard tenant step draws the scan key, management client secret and Gateway workspace key as text fields (`R2Onboard` `f.scanKey`, `f.secret`, `f.gwKey`) | "Add scan key…" button opens the host's native secret sheet; the step then shows a masked indicator and the vault record id | The web layer never holds a secret (D014, C09 reveal ownership). Visual delta: button plus masked state instead of a field |
| P2 | Rotation panel renders the secret in a readonly input with Reveal/Copy (`R2App` `secretShown`) | Reveal shows the secret in the host secret sheet; Copy goes through the host clipboard; both consume a one-time disclosure token | Invariant I6; same reason as P1 |
| P3 | State lives in one component with `byTenant` (`R2App` `TS/ts/tPatch`) | Core-owned scopes per [state-ownership.md](state-ownership.md) | D014 authority; prototype state model is normative for invariants only |
| P4 | ⌘1–⌘9 are advertised but not bound (canvas rule: no global key handlers) | Native menu defines the shortcuts and dispatches `navigate` | Host owns menus; works under VoiceOver and shows in the menu bar |
| P5 | Sidebar lists Home, Inbox, Harness, Academy, Test Cases, API Client, Administration | Same, backed by Rust feature ids `home`, `inbox`, `harness`, `academy`, `library`, `apiClient`, `administration`; `gateway` and `redTeam` move inside Administration; `sessions`, `import`, `settings` reachable from the palette and Administration | One Rust inventory feeds menu, sidebar and palette |
| P6 | Wizard boards are 1440×900 with a fixed 320px rail | Minimum window 1180×720; rail collapses to icons below 1280 wide | 13-inch displays |
| P7 | Toasts expire on timers always | Persist until dismissed under Reduce Motion | Native acceptance Reduce Motion check |
| P8 | Inbox items, Harness lifecycles, API responses, cases and Academy progress are invented in-component data | Wire fixtures in `packages/desktop-contracts/fixtures/wire`, content derived from the prototype data | Fixtures are the contract test surface; prototype data is never shipped |
| P9 | `TOPICS.injo` added in round 16 as a separate key from detector `inj` | Core topic inventory keys are a distinct type from detector ids | Carries the round-16 fix into the type system |
| P10 | Administration creation label is a per-product single value | Per-tab creation capability and label in `AdminInventory` | Round 16 finding: wrong label on API keys, Security profiles and Logs tabs |
| P11 | Home snooze button hard-codes "Snooze 24 hours" | Item-specific `snoozeLabel` shared by Home and Inbox | Round 16 finding; invariant I11 |
| P12 | API Client Send has no live completion status | Compact `role="status"` region with request name, response code and duration; JSON body outside the live region | Round 16 finding |
| P13 | Tenant switch with an open applied key review leaves disclosure open | Any review exit, including switch, finalises disclosure | Round 16 finding; invariant I6 |
| P14 | Applied injection-override examples show "Not demonstrated" in the Playground | Topic examples classify under the topic key with a profile-aware verdict | Round 16 finding |
| P15 | Import from v1 is a separate destination | Also offered on the wizard tenant step as a secondary action; credentials re-entered via the secret sheet | First run is where an upgrading user needs it |
| P16 | Harness lifecycle 2 (mcp) reads an AES endpoint inventory (`CONVOS.mcp.tool` = `aes-inventory/get_endpoints`) and its follow-up is an AES policy review | The fixture reads the workspace MCP server inventory the core already exposes (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, C22) and the follow-up opens a Gateway guardrail review (`ChangeKind::GatewayGuardrails`) | AES has no admitted service (P18); the lifecycle stays read-only as in the prototype |
| P17 | Wizard tenant step requires a management OAuth client id and secret (`ERR.clientId`, `ERR.secret`) and makes the scan key optional (`scanOn`) | First run requires the scan key and makes the management client an optional second section; add mode keeps both optional. Secrets enter only through the host secret sheet | D013 (scan-key-first; management credentials are separate and never imply scan authentication) |
| P18 | Six products are verified, inventoried and administered (AIS tool policies, AES discovered AI and policies, AES policy review `REVIEWS.aes`) | Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or changed: Runtime Security (`ManagementResource`, `ChangeKind`), Gateway (`GatewayRead`, Gateway `ChangeKind`s), Red Team and Model/skill reads (C13, read-only). Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as "Not available in this release" with their dependency gate; no inventory, verification result or mutation is invented for them | The existing interfaces do not supply those product APIs; fixtures cannot establish a capability (parity matrix, C13 rule) |
| P19 | Wizard Academy step creates a local account with streaks, reminders and a quiz (`s.acad`, `s.af`, `s.streaks`, `s.remind`, `s.quiz`) | The step signs in to the hosted Academy identity or skips; the embedded learner view owns progress and gamification | D004–D006, C04 |
| P20 | Key rotation issues a new key with a 24-hour grace period, a later revocation and a secret recoverable from the Keychain (`REVIEWS.key`, `keyRotated/keySecretClosed/keyDeployed/keyDoneAt`) | Two admitted operations in sequence: `ApiKeys · Version` (additional key, one-time reveal through the existing `RevealSecret` host sheet, never persisted) → local deployment acknowledgement → `ApiKeys · Delete` of the old key with readback. Two distinct timestamps; a lost secret means "issue another key" | `changes.rs` 842–846 and `management.rs` 980–988 define Rotate (regenerate, no grace) and Version (additional key); DESIGN.md "Secrets" forbids persisting issued keys |
| P21 | Test Cases "Run" was described in the first spec draft as scanning any case | Runtime-only "Open in Playground"; "Inspect" for every other product, which never dispatches a scan | Prototype lines 1035–1037 and the parity matrix (no invented execution) |
| P22 | Red Team Targets tab lists target names (`rtm` tables) | Targets shows `target_id` and `target_type` only | The C13 contract (`red_team.rs` 5–8, 56–66) deliberately keeps target names out of the core's reads; the privacy boundary is not widened for presentation |
| P23 | One rotation flow for every key | Staged Version → Delete rotation for Runtime API keys only; Gateway keys use `Rotate` (regenerate in place) | `gateway_changes::allowed` admits Rotate, not Version, for Gateway keys |
| P24 | Minimum window 1180×720 (DESIGN-V2 §4, tokens.md) | The host keeps `min_inner_size(900, 600)` until C34 because C01-N09 (re-run cumulatively by C24-N06) pins the legacy minimum; the v2 shell is laid out to stay usable without horizontal scroll at 900×600 (rail collapsed to icons below 1280 CSS px) and C28-N07 asserts it at that size. C34 raises the minimum together with the legacy-script retirement | Legacy native acceptance must keep passing while both shells exist (D058 §5) |
| P25 | Revision of P1/P2: "secrets enter only through a host secret sheet" | The retained, reviewed entry path (C02/C09) is a webview password field read through a ref, cleared immediately and sent once inside the intent as `EnteredSecret` (zeroized, never in React state, never logged); the host owns the *reveal* sheet (`RevealSecret`) and the clipboard. C29 uses exactly this path for the scan key, management secret and Gateway key; no new host entry sheet is built | The C27 spec assumed an entry sheet the host does not have; the reviewed pattern already satisfies D014 and the C24 canary checks (no secret in any AX tree or file) |
| P26 | `verifyTenantSetup` as one core intent streaming `VerifyStep`s (wizard.md) | Implemented as specified: `verifyTenantSetup{context}` runs in the core over the draft, admits the reads it needs through the management pipeline (management verify; the chosen Gateway workspace's prerequisites, or only the listing when none is chosen, reported as incomplete coverage; Red Team; model/skill) and settles each line from the result; AIS/AES are listed as not available and never read; the lines, `done`, `failed`, `blocking` and `stale` stream through `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify`. The scan key has no admitted read: its line is `stored`, never `pass`. Only the scan key (first run) blocks; an optional service's failure is carried as a warning the operator may continue over | Attempt-01 composed the reads in the presentation; attempt-02 read only the workspace listing and blocked on every failure — both rejected |
| P27 | Add-tenant Cancel writes nothing except an already-vaulted credential | The wizard works on a core-owned draft (`beginTenantSetup`) with its own context and its own Gateway listing and choice; credentials attach to the draft; nothing is listed as a tenant (a draft id is refused by `selectTenant`/`deleteTenant`), the active tenant and its Gateway choice are untouched until `completeTenantSetup` commits the draft in one step and switches once. Every change to the draft's credentials or profile retires its context (and the binding, reads and listing under it). Cancel (`discardTenantSetup`) drops the draft, retires the binding and every read under its context and workspace reference, and queues its Keychain records for cleanup; the sheet closes only once the core admits it (a creation still pending or unconfirmed is awaited and whatever it produced is discarded). A relaunch, or a tenant committed outside the wizard during first run, does the same | Stronger than the spec's leftover credential: no orphan secrets and no cached authority surviving a replaced credential |
| P28 | Gateway workspace key entered on the wizard tenant step | Implemented: an optional section reads the workspaces through the management client (`management` gateway inventory on the draft context), `selectGatewayWorkspace` chooses one, and `configureInferenceCredential` saves the endpoint and key for it | The admitted sequence (list → select → key) runs on the draft like on a tenant |
| P29 | "Import from v1" fills the tenant step in place | The action navigates to the reviewed import destination (C23), which renders on its own during first run with a 'Back to setup' control; the wizard resumes afterwards | The import review flow is itself reviewed and must not be duplicated inside the wizard |
| P30 | The wizard opens whenever onboarding is not done | The shell shows onboarding as done whenever a tenant already exists (an upgrade, or a tenant created before the v2 shell was chosen); the stored state is untouched. A first run is one with no tenant yet; a relaunch mid-setup reopens at the Tenant step with a fresh draft (the previous draft's credentials are cleaned up) | Existing users must never be sent through the wizard; a half-made draft is never a tenant |
| P31 | Products step shows licence state read from the tenant | No admitted service reports licensing (DESIGN-V2 §6); each card shows what it reads with (scan key / management client, saved or not) and, once verified, that product's verify result; a note says licence state is not readable through the public APIs | Nothing may be inferred about licensing from reads that do not report it |
| P32 | First-run import returns to the wizard | A v1 import during first run commits its tenants through the reviewed C23 flow; the desktop then has a tenant, so the first-run draft ends (its credentials are cleaned up) and the shell opens on the imported tenant; its scan key is added in Administration (the reviewed C08/C09 credential setup) | A tenant that exists is never sent back through a first-run wizard (P30), and the wizard cannot wrap an existing tenant without duplicating it |
| P33 | `ReadStatus` as its own core scope with per-product states from dedicated reads | `readTenant` admits one finite read per chosen product through the existing management pipeline: the Runtime overview the shell already owns (sessions chart, key inventory, the chosen Gateway workspace's usage and limits), the Red Team statistics and scan list (C13's read), the model scans, skill statistics and skill scans (C13's read); each settles into the tenant's read record as a per-source outcome (read, needs a role, failed); a source counts as read only when it established what it is for: a listing whose page is the inventory (not capped, nothing beyond the first page), a sessions chart with consistent totals, a key page with every expiration reported, Gateway usage with the day's requests and errors both known; anything less is named in `sourcesMissing` (the same gaps the Inbox shows). A product attempt that established nothing (every source failed, or the read never reached the service) is kept as the latest failed attempt beside the product's last outcome that did, which stays shown as older; the read is failed while such an attempt is the latest (a missing role is a settled attempt reported on the product, not a failed read). Once anything was attempted, every chosen source nothing has reached yet is named as unread coverage too, and `settledAt` carries the time of the latest attempt so a completion that established nothing is announced for when it settled, never as a first read still pending. A healthy empty read older than the ten-minute window (`OverviewView.fresh`, C12) is a gap: Home and the Inbox keep the cached read and name its age instead of an all-clear, never re-reading on their own; a refresh in progress keeps that read and its age named until the new read lands (admission restores nothing). A chosen Gateway with no workspace is unread coverage, named in `sourcesMissing`; `recheckProduct` re-reads one chosen product. The read completes (one generation) when every part admitted under the tenant's current context has landed; a read in which no source answered is a failed read (`failed`, with `failedAt`), never pending and never done, and a failed refresh keeps the earlier read labelled as older | No second read pipeline, and Home never claims coverage a read did not establish (I4): revised after the C30 attempt-01 review, which found selected Red Team and model/skill products left unread and a workspace-less Gateway announced as complete |
| P34 | Inbox items with `kind`, `severity` and `explanation` per item | `InboxEntry` carries the overview item (Runtime, Gateway) or the C29 setup warning with its hold (listed, with every action, whatever the read status: a warning exists before any read); the explanation is per kind in the presentation's copy, not invented per item; `deadline` is absent (P20). Its action carries an `InboxTarget` (destination, product, Administration inventory, item name): a key item opens Administration on the API keys inventory filtered to the key, a setup warning opens where it is fixed (Settings for the scan key or management client, AI Gateway, AI Red Teaming) | The admitted reads report no per-item explanation or deadline; the target was added after the attempt-01 review found actions landing on the destination's default view |
| P35 | Guard offers "Archive and switch" / "Discard and switch" | Archive ends the conversation (its protected history stays as C20 saved it); Discard ends it, removes its protected history record (after any save under way, under the same lock the sink holds) and refuses every later save of it (the turn the stop cuts short), then drops it once its task returns; both then switch once; the pending approval is never decided for the operator; the dialog's initial focus is Stay, the safe default (Enter on arrival ends nothing). A save admitted before the discard (the turn the engine's end cut short) re-checks the tombstone under the history hold before it writes, so it can never write the discarded transcript back after the removal. A removal that fails (storage unavailable, access denied) is never implied to have succeeded: the record stays listed and the workspace's history reports the failure, through unrelated opens and removals, until the operator removes the record | The engine is stopped either way: a switch retires its binding (C20); the difference is whether the transcript is kept, which the dialog promises and the attempt-01 review found unkept |
| P36 | Completing "Add a tenant" switches to the new tenant | `completeTenantSetup{resolution?}` goes through the same guard as `selectTenant`: a pending Harness decision refuses it with `switchBlocked{to: <draft id>, completion: true}`, nothing is committed or retired, the draft stays, and the wizard renders the one guard dialog and re-sends the completion with the resolution | Wizard completion is listed among the entry points of the single guarded switch path (shell.md, I1); the attempt-01 review found it bypassing the guard |
| P37 | Scopes are per tenant and a switch round trip shows the cached state | Each opened tenant owns its read record in the core (its last overview, the other products' outcomes, generation, recheck, snooze receipt) for the session; a switch away and back shows it again without a new read. Per-operation bookkeeping is separate from the cached data: each read part is tracked with the context it was admitted under, "reading" means a part under the current context is open, a part under a retired context is withdrawn when it lands (never recorded, never counted), so a read held open across a switch round trip can never leave the tenant reading. The presentation's own transient state follows the tenant too: the shell store carries the shown scope (set from each snapshot before it renders); a toast for a tenant's item or read and an unconfirmed delivery bound to a context are refused at insertion when that scope is no longer shown (a refusal or lost delivery arriving after a switch), retired on leaving it (however long Reduce Motion would keep them) and filtered at render; a contextual arrival belongs to the tenant it was followed in | I2 names the tenant, not the context, as the owner of its cached scopes; the attempt-01 review found the overview bound to the context and lost across A → B → A |
| P38 | Snooze toast states the core-computed return time | The core issues a `SnoozeReceipt{id, title, returnsAt, sequence}` on the tenant's Inbox scope for the last snooze placed in the session; the shell announces a new sequence once, in the tenant it was placed in, and never a hold a switch or a reread merely rediscovers; a snooze ends at its return time whether or not its entry is actionable, and the core's deadline ticker publishes the expiry (the holds in effect are part of the overview's time signature) | I11 wants one source for the return time and one announcement per snooze; diffing holds between snapshots announced old holds after a switch (attempt-01 review) |
| P39 | `AdminInventory` generated from screens/administration-inventory.md | Implemented as a Rust table (`admin_inventory::inventory`) carried on `ShellSnapshot.administration` in the v2 shell only (null in the legacy shell, whose literal snapshot and leak canary are unchanged; the presentation's fixture copy is pinned to the table by a core test); a unit test and the `ui_v2_reviews` suite pin every offered (kind, action) to `changes::is_allowed` and every denied pair to its refusal, so a label can never outrun the core. Read and Change tabs render the retained views (the same reads and reviewed changes) embedded under the accordion with the inventory's labels; Unavailable tabs and products show their gate and nothing else; Removed tabs are not rendered. The Gateway MCP servers tab lists the chosen workspace's catalog through `listMcpServers` | One source of truth for dispositions, checked against the matrices the core enforces (V2-T20, V2-R11) |
| P40 | `openReview`/`confirmReview{typed}` intents | The retained review intents are the review (`reviewChange` binds tenant, context, credentials and the read baseline; `requestApply{confirmation}` validates the typed name in the core and opens the native confirmation, whose private completion alone applies). The v2 sheet disables Apply until the typed text equals the review's name and renders the applied panel only from the core's result and readback | The C09 review already carries every binding I3 asks for; new intents would duplicate it |
| P41 | Rotation state per tenant with two stored timestamps | `Tenant.rotations` (persisted, never a secret): driven from settled `ApiKeys` results of the active context (a Version read back starts one; an unknown Version starts an unconfirmed one; a failed one records nothing; the old key's Delete read back absent completes it; a rejected Delete keeps it with the reason; an unknown one is reconciled by a complete key inventory read), plus `acknowledgeDeployment{application}` stored once. The reminder is the Inbox item `rotation-<application>` while a key is confirmed and the old one not read back gone. The one-time secret stays on its change result for the context it was applied in: Reveal is offered only while the core holds it; any sheet exit forgets it and a context change drops it | The issued secret must never be persisted (I6) while the stage and timestamps must survive reads, switches and relaunches (I7) |
| P42 | Playground topic examples classify under the topic | The scan result carries `topics{violation, blocked, allowed}` from the service's `topic_violation` flag and `topic_guardrails_details`, shown apart from the detector flags: a matched topic is "flagged (blocked)" or "flagged (monitor only)", no match under the profile is "Not detected", an unreported verdict says so; detectors stay listed beside it. The prototype's local classifier is not carried: verdicts come from the service | The desktop never invents a verdict (DESIGN-V2 §10 gap 2 closed with service data) |
| P43 | C31 live row runs through `xtask live-run` | `live-run --family L03 --subject C31` runs the C31 topic routine inside L03's designation and writes its receipts and run record under evidence/C31/live, leaving C25's receipts untouched (the consumption ledger stays C25's single ledger) | The live row needs the reviewed runner and the owner's designation, not a second harness |
| P44 | Lifecycle conversations are seeded data in the prototype (`CONVOS`) | The three lifecycles are fixtures in the contracts package (`fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-{blocks,mcp,keys}.json`), one `ConversationView` per step (asking, approved, declined, ended) plus the saved transcript. Each read is one of the desktop's own read tools (`desktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`): blocks `search`, mcp `mcpServers`, keys `inventory` of API keys. They are regression inputs and boards only: a real tenant never shows them (I12) | The core decides approvals; fixtures must be shapes the core emits, validated by `parseShellSnapshot` |
| P45 | The mcp and keys follow-ups open a guardrail review and the key rotation flow | `proposeChange` admits, besides the Runtime and DLP kinds, an API key `version` (the hand-off of a rotation: it opens the C31 review sheet and its staged sequence) and a guardrail of the conversation's own Gateway workspace (create or update only), staged with that workspace's reference so its review is bound to it; Gateway deletes and every other Gateway change, and a key rotation in place, stay refused. The follow-up of a key hand-off shows the rotation stage from `ShellSnapshot.rotations`. The mcp lifecycle's guardrail is described as what it is (a pii-check guardrail on all messages while the unlisted server is reviewed); no guardrail expresses an MCP allow list | A proposal only ever opens the operator's own review (D014); the follow-ups must be core-owned, not page-invented |
| P46 | `endConversation` returns an archive id | `endHarness` keeps its receipt; the ended conversation names its saved transcript (`ConversationView.historyId`, the protected history record, shown only with save evidence: `savedAt` set and no `saveFailure`) and the rail marks it ended; "Open the saved transcript" opens that record | The archive already exists as the C20 protected history; a second id would duplicate it |
| P47 | The mcp lifecycle reads `GatewayRead::McpServers` (P16) | A new desktop read tool `mcpServers` reads the conversation workspace's MCP catalog (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, the read the MCP panel uses) for the engine, decided in the page like every desktop read | The prototype's AES inventory has no admitted service; the Gateway catalog is the admitted read P16 named |
| P48 | Read-only tool calls are approved in the page (C21 assumed) | Every desktop read tool (inventory, detail, session, search, Red Teaming, model observation, scan, test cases, MCP catalog) now waits for the operator's decision in the page (`ApprovalKind::DesktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`); declined, nothing is read and the engine is told so. Staging a proposal reads nothing and still runs at once. Before C32 these reads ran without asking | The lifecycles' approve and decline paths, and the operator's control over what tenant data reaches the model |
| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), whose private completion alone sends the allow; refused, the decision stays pending; a stale one is dismissed. In TypeScript `effect` is optional only so pre-C32 snapshots type-check, and absent is treated as `mayChange` | In-page consent must never authorise a change (D058, C32) |
| P50 | A composer draft per conversation | One composer per tenant and workspace (by the stable workspace ID): its draft survives navigation, tenant round trips and the retirement of the conversation it was typed for. A message the core did not take is kept under "Not sent"; one whose delivery was not confirmed under "Not confirmed". Each send carries the presentation's own ID (`sendHarness.submission`, 32 hex): the core takes each ID at most once (a repeated delivery after a lost receipt sends nothing again) and reports the sends it took with the message each became and where each stands (`ConversationView.submissions`, newest 64): `pending` (taken, a sign-in renewal or the write confirmation under way: it may still be given back), `delivered` (its message entered the transcript, recorded by the engine session apart from the transcript's bounds) or `returned` (given back, or failed without an engine). Only `delivered` and `returned` settle anything; a tracked send whose conversation is no longer shown before its final word (retired, cleared, another tenant or workspace meanwhile) is kept as Not confirmed with its text. A "Not confirmed" record is settled only by its own conversation reporting its own ID: delivered, it leaves the list (and the box, if it holds exactly that text); given back, it leaves the list and its exact text stays in the box or is kept under Not sent, never cleared; an admitted send is likewise tracked until its conversation reports it, and its receipt never clears text the core already gave back, whichever arrives first; never by matching text, another send's entry, another conversation, or the transcript (which drops its oldest entries past 200 entries or 1 MiB). The core numbers a conversation's messages and never reuses an ID, also when restored. Nothing is sent by itself | A conversation's draft must stay recoverable after the core retires the conversation (C32 reviews) |
| P51 | A new investigation prefilled with the ended conversation's opening question | The rail titles the current conversation from its kept opening too, so its title never changes as the transcript drops its oldest entries. A contextual arrival stays pending, bound to its tenant and workspace, until the conversation or the history resolves it ("Finding that investigation…" while the saved conversations are listed); listed without it, or not listable, the page says so; navigation, a new arrival, another workspace or a restart cancels it. The opening question is the conversation's opening message, whole, kept by the core apart from its title (a one-line, shortened label for lists): `ConversationView.opening` and `OpenedConversationView.opening`, captured when the first message (`u-1`) enters the transcript, saved with the record, restored with it and kept after the transcript drops its oldest entries. An archived conversation's record is read for it when the restart is asked for (nothing is filled in meanwhile); choosing another investigation, sending, opening a transcript, a new arrival or another workspace retires that read (a late result changes nothing); otherwise it settles by the core's receipt for the record (`HarnessHistoryView.lastOpen`: id, admission order, failure), newer than any open settled before it was asked for, never by a busy edge or a changed error. When it is not known (a record saved before C32 whose transcript no longer holds its first message) or the record cannot be read, the page says so and never fills in the title instead; a draft in the box is kept under Not sent and nothing is sent | The title is shortened (72 or 80 characters, one line) and lost the rest of a long or multi-line question; the attempt-12 review found the restart prefilling it |
| P52 | The API Client re-skinned to the prototype's layout (request tree, tabs, method/URL line, 40% response pane) | The v2 API Client presents the C14/C15 workbench in the v2 layout through a layout context its wrapper provides (`shell/presentation.ts` `V2Layout`): the page heading becomes the v2 title row (no kicker), and the request editor puts the method, URL, Send and Save on one compact line with the request details beside a response pane (stacked when narrow). Collections, tabs, history, reviews and execution are the existing components and authority, unchanged; the legacy shell keeps its layout. Send's completion is announced in the editor's status region as "<request> · <code> · <duration>", the body outside it, in the response pane (known gap 5, V2-T14). The collection tree and tab strip keep the workbench's own arrangement | The workbench view itself is held by uncommitted owner work in this tree and is not edited; its layout is changed only through the components it renders |
| P53 | "Generic requests … refused by the core as today" (screens/api-client-test-cases.md) | Unchanged C15 authority: a request to a known tenant operation that would change configuration needs its exact, natively confirmed review (`ReviewRequired`) before it is sent; an unknown operation at a registered origin is refused (`UnknownEndpoint`) | The screen text summarised both as refused; the core's existing rule is the authority and C33 does not change it |
| P54 | Show the calls reveals each action's request and response "as the diagnostics module already redacts them" | The diagnostics module records typed events only. The core now records, in memory for the app run, each call it makes on a tenant's behalf through its transport (scans, management reads and changes with their token requests, AI Gateway inference): purpose, the credential by kind (the core attaches it), method, origin and path without the query (a query is marked withheld), header names (never values), request size, status or failure, duration and response content type, newest 50 per tenant (`ShellSnapshot.calls`, only while the preference is on; forgotten with the tenant), bounded as the contract accepts them (header names of at most 64 characters, at most 128, path cut at 2048 characters, method of at most 16, status 100-999, HTTPS origins only; `CALL_*_LIMIT`). Bodies are never kept: a response can carry an issued key or a token and requests carry tenant content. The API Client's own requests keep their existing redacted history. The preference is the operator's once chosen (`show_calls_chosen`), otherwise on until onboarding completes | The prototype has no calls panel; DESIGN-V2 §3 asked for it; secrets must never reach a snapshot (I6) |
| P55 | Test Cases with per-case results and pack names (prototype `results`, `packCases`) | A case carries its starter pack's name (set when the bundled catalog's cases are added, at onboarding completion or by Add starter packs) and its last result: the verdict of a Playground scan opened from the case, recorded by the core when the scan settles and only while the case still holds the prompt and response that were scanned (`TestCase.result`, `Intent::Scan.case`). Neither is ever exported or imported (v1's file format is unchanged); a copy to another tenant drops the result. A case can be named by a scan only if it is a Runtime Security case of the tenant's open library (otherwise the scan is refused before anything is sent); a result stored on any other case is dropped when the library is read. Absent result: Not run. The library's workflows are kept in the v2 screen: New case, Add starter packs, Add public examples, Import…, Export…, Remove and explicit Copy to another tenant, with the library's unsaved, failure, problem and notice states and their recovery (Save again, Open the library again). Search and the product filter run over name, pack and input; pages of 20 (at most 32 cases per tenant, v1's limit) | The library had no result or pack; the prototype's results were UI state |
| P56 | "Open in Playground" sets the Playground's prompt, direction and profile | It hands the case's profile, prompt, response and application to Administration's Runtime Playground (the v2 home of the Runtime scan form) and opens that tab; the operator runs the scan there, and that scan names the case. The scan form has no separate direction: a case's prompt and response are its directions. Nothing is scanned by opening it, and Inspect dispatches nothing | The Runtime scan form (C03) is the admitted execution surface; direction is expressed by which fields hold text |

P10–P14 are the five round-16 Codex items carried as known gaps (see
[DESIGN-V2.md](DESIGN-V2.md) "Known gaps"). P16–P21 were added after the C27
attempt-01 review (R3–R6); P22–P23 after attempt-03 (F3a, F3b); P24–P25 were recorded in C28 and P26–P32 in C29 and P33–P38 in C30 and P39–P43 in C31, P44–P51 in C32 and P52–P56 in C33 when implementation met the host as it is (P26–P28 revised after the C29 attempt-01 review; P26–P27 again, and P32 added, after attempt-02; P33–P35 revised and P36–P38 added after the C30 attempt-01 review).
# P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen

Agreed with the owner on 2026-10-29 after C32 took twenty review rounds while
the built v2 screens never matched the design. This supersedes the chunking
of C33–C34 in `IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md`; the specification
(`docs/architecture/ui-v2/`) and the core work of C27–C33 stand.

## The bar

- **The prototype is the spec.** `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` (Refined
  Native v2, version 134, checksums in `MANIFEST.sha256`) is the reference for
  every screen; the canvas is not consulted.
- **A screen is done when it matches its board.** Side by side at 1440 wide in
  light and dark (the board is drawn at 1440×900; where the display cannot
  give a 900px web area both sides are rendered at the window's measured web
  area, named in the composite's caption, 1440×800 on the current Mac), with
  real core data wired, and the fast checks (typecheck,
  Vitest, the affected Rust suites) passing. Parity is structural: regions,
  widths, spacing, type scale, tokens and component shapes. Content differs;
  accessibility corrections (focus rings, contrast, status regions) may deviate
  and are noted. The owner judges each composite.
- **Composites** live at `docs/planning/evidence/<chunk>/parity/<board>-<theme>.png`
  (prototype left, app right) with `notes.md` listing deviations, produced by
  `tools/parity/compose.py` (headless render of the prototype board beside the
  fixture app's own capture).
- **One heavy gate per chunk**, after the owner's parity approval: rebuild,
  native acceptance, the full Rust fixture suite and the Codex review, accepted
  at **8**. The phase review (after C44) is **9**; the C29 live Vulture row runs
  before it once the owner designates the L19 family.
- **No legacy views inside v2.** Each screen is new components over the
  existing core intents and `ShellSnapshot`; the legacy feature components stay
  untouched for the legacy shell until C44 removes it.
- **Cadence.** The shell is approved before any screen inside it. After that
  a chunk's composite is posted and work moves to the next chunk; the owner's
  notes are applied as they arrive.

## Chunks

| Chunk | Screen | Board(s) | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| C33 | core: case results and packs, call log, submissions | — | committed without a receipt; its two screens are superseded by C40/C41 |
| C35 | Shell | R2App any start | 56px header (title, 260px search, Explain switch = `showCalls`, Harness button); nav with product children and badges; status block; tenant menu 360px; palette 640px; tokens: text 13px, gaps, scrims, header/section/overlay sizes; Classic switch moves to Settings |
| C36 | Home | start=home | 3-column product cards with sparklines; Needs attention beside Quick actions |
| C37 | Inbox | start=inbox | fix-up: 430px list column, 3-column facts grid |
| C38 | Harness | start=harness (+ convo boards) | title band; 244px rail; transcript with 46px composer; 350px Decisions aside; own setup panels |
| C39a | Administration: Runtime Security | start=admin | section + tab strip; overview stat grid with sparkline; Playground form; generic tables over the management reads |
| C39b | Administration: other products | start=admin, start=review | the remaining products on the same pattern; the review sheet (620px) |
| C40 | Test Cases | start=cases | explain banner; one table card; packs and new-case dialogs (560px); detail sheet (520px) |
| C41 | API Client | start=api | full port: title row with chips; 286px request tree; open-request tabs; request sub-tabs; response pane |
